.TH iiod 1 "@CMAKE_DATE@" "libiio-@LIBIIO_VERSION_MAJOR@.@LIBIIO_VERSION_MINOR@" "LibIIO Utilities"
.IX iiod
.SH NAME
iiod \- IIO Daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iiod
[
.I options
]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iiod
is a server built on top of Libiio which can share a Libiio context across the
network, USB, or a UART link.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Display the version of this program.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-debug
Output debug log to the standard output.
.TP
.B \-D, \-\-demux
Demux channels directly on the server.
.TP
.B \-F, \-\-ffs <arg>
Use the given FunctionFS mountpoint to serve over USB.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-nb\-pipes <arg>
Specify the number of USB pipes (ep couples) to use.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-serial <arg>
Run iiod on the specified UART.
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-port <arg>
Port to listen on (default = 30431).
Using --port 0 will pick an ephemeral port (dynamic / unused in the range between 32768–60999).
.TP
.B \-U, \-\-udp\-port <arg>
Serve UDP sample streams (Linux only), with the datagrams sent from the given
UDP port. Without this option, a client's request for a UDP sample stream is
refused and samples are served over the connection as usual.
See
.B UDP SAMPLE STREAMS
below.
.TP
.B \-B, \-\-udp\-block\-size <arg>
Size in bytes of the blocks that UDP sample streams are read in from the
device (default = 1048576). It is rounded down to a whole number of datagrams.
.TP
.B \-u, \-\-uri
The Uniform Resource Identifier
.I (uri)
for connecting to devices, can be one of:
.RS
.IP ip:[address]
network address, either numeric (192.168.0.1) or network hostname
.IP ip:
blank, if compiled with zeroconf support, will find an IIO device on network
.IP usb:[device:port:instance]
normally returned from
.B iio_info -S
.IP serial:[port],[baud],[settings]
which are controlled, and need to match the iiod (or tinyiiod) on the other end of the serial port.
.RS
.IP [port]
is something like '/dev/ttyUSB0' on Linux, and 'COM4' on Windows.
.IP [baud]
is normally one of 110, 300, 600, 1200, 2400, 4800, 9600, 14400, 19200, 38400, 57600, 115200 [default], 128000 or 256000, but can vary system to system.
.IP [settings]
would normally be configured as '8n1' this is controlled by:
.RS
.IP data_bits:
(5, 6, 7, 8 [default], or 9)
.IP parity_bits:
('n' none [default], 'o' odd, 'e' even, 'm' mark, or 's' space)
.IP stop_bits:
(1 [default, or 2)
.IP flow_control:
('0' none [default], 'x' Xon Xoff, 'r' RTSCTS, or 'd' DTRDSR)
.RE
.RE
.IP local:
with no address part. This is the default.
.RE
.SH UDP SAMPLE STREAMS
With
.BR \-\-udp\-port ,
the context that
.B iiod
serves carries the attribute
.I iiod,udp-stream
and a client may ask for the samples of an input buffer as UDP datagrams,
which is what the
.I daqiri:
backend of Libiio does. Everything else, the request included, still goes
through the network connection.
.PP
The client names the address and port the datagrams go to and the largest
payload it accepts.
.B iiod
answers with the source address and port, a stream identifier and the payload
size: the largest multiple of 16 bytes and of the sample size that fits. While
the buffer is enabled, a thread reads blocks from the device and sends each as
a run of datagrams made of a 16-byte header (version, flags, payload length,
stream identifier, 64-bit sequence number) and that many bytes of samples.
The datagrams are neither encrypted nor authenticated.
.PP
Nothing paces the stream. When the device produces samples faster than they
can be sent, it overruns between blocks: the sequence numbers show no gap, but
the samples of one block do not follow those of the block before. Only input
buffers are served. A request for another payload size is refused with EBUSY
while the blocks of an earlier request are still queued, which they are until
a stream has run or the buffer is closed.
.PP
.B iiod \-\-udp\-port 25102
.TP

.SH RETURN VALUE
If the specified device is not found, a non-zero exit code is returned.
